  <h1>What to Do After Workplace Harassment</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Riley Chen (RC), Employment &amp; Civil Rights Practice Lead. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>1. Document every incident</h2><p>Record date, time, location, exact words or actions, witnesses, and your response. Use personal email or a private document — not company systems. Keep copies at home. This contemporaneous record is your most powerful evidence.</p>
  <h2>2. Report through internal channels</h2><p>Follow your employer&rsquo;s anti-harassment policy. Report to HR or a designated officer. Put the complaint in writing (email is fine). This creates a record, triggers the employer&rsquo;s investigation obligation, and defeats the Faragher/Ellerth defense if you later sue. Failure to report can bar your claim in some contexts.</p>
  <h2>3. File an EEOC charge</h2><p>File within 180 days (300 in states with fair employment agencies) of each discriminatory act. Filing online at eeoc.gov is easiest. The EEOC will contact the employer and investigate. Request a right-to-sue letter if the EEOC doesn&rsquo;t act within 180 days of your charge.</p>
  <h2>4. Consult an employment attorney</h2><p>Do this early. An attorney can help you document the claim, advise on whether to use mediation (EEOC offers free mediation), and evaluate your damages. Most employment attorneys take Title VII cases on contingency.</p>
  <h2>Where to go from here</h2>
  <p>See <a href="/guides/how-hostile-work-environment-claims-work">how claims work</a> and <a href="/guides/hostile-work-misconceptions">common misconceptions</a>.</p>
